## Why This Is Asked

A practical exercise to gauge understanding of rate limiting, multi-tenant isolation, and backpressure in an LLM gateway.

## Key Concepts

- Token-bucket rate limiting
- Per-tenant quotas
- Backpressure and fairness
- Observability and tracing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: simple token bucket for a tenant
class Bucket {
  constructor(capacity, refillPerMs) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.refillPerMs = refillPerMs;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  take(n = 1) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = now - this.last;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.refillPerMs);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would bucket state persist across restarts?
- How would you scale this across many gateway instances?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical data provenance and tamper detection in multi-tenant LLM gateways.

## Key Concepts
- Data provenance and tamper-evident logging
- Per-tenant keys and HMAC signing
- Privacy-aware logging and redaction
- Simple retention and rotation policies

## Code Example
```javascript
const crypto = require('crypto');
function signLogLine(line, key){
  const h = crypto.createHmac('sha256', key);
  h.update(line);
  return h.digest('hex');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate tenant keys without breaking logs?
- How would you prove log integrity to auditors without exposing PII?
